  <main>
   <h2 class=3D"heading no-num settled" id=3D"goals"><span class=3D"content=
">Goals</span></h2>
   <ul>
    <li>
     <p>Deduplicate boilerplate in standards.

 </p>
    </li><li>
     <p>Align standards on conventions, terminology, and data structures.

 </p>
    </li><li>
     <p>Be a place for concepts used by multiple standards without a good h=
ome.

 </p>
    </li><li>
     <p>Help write clear and readable algorithmic prose by clarifying other=
wise ambiguous concepts.
</p>
   </li></ul>
   <p>Suggestions for more goals welcome.</p>
   <h2 class=3D"heading settled" data-level=3D"1" id=3D"usage"><span class=
=3D"secno">1. </span><span class=3D"content">Usage</span><a class=3D"self-l=
ink" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#usage"></a></h2>
   <p>To make use of this standard in a document titled <var>X</var>, use:

</p>
   <p><samp><var>X</var> depends on <cite>Infra</cite>. <a data-link-type=
=3D"biblio" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#biblio-infra" title=3D"I=
nfra Standard">[Infra]</a></samp>

</p>
   <p>Additionally, cross-referencing all terminology is strongly encourage=
d to avoid ambiguity.


</p>
   <h2 class=3D"heading settled" data-level=3D"2" id=3D"conventions"><span =
class=3D"secno">2. </span><span class=3D"content">Conventions</span><a clas=
s=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#conventions"></a></h=
2>
   <h3 class=3D"heading settled" data-level=3D"2.1" id=3D"conformance"><spa=
n class=3D"secno">2.1. </span><span class=3D"content">Conformance</span><a =
class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#conformance"></a=
></h3>
   <p>All assertions, diagrams, examples, and notes are non-normative, as a=
re all sections explicitly
marked non-normative. Everything else is normative.

</p>
   <p>The keywords "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL NOT", "S=
HOULD", "SHOULD NOT",
"RECOMMENDED", "NOT RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" are to be interpret=
ed as described in
RFC 2119. <a data-link-type=3D"biblio" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.or=
g/#biblio-rfc2119" title=3D"Key words for use in RFCs to Indicate Requireme=
nt Levels">[RFC2119]</a>

</p>
   <p>These keywords have equivalent meaning when written in lowercase and =
cannot appear in
non-normative content.

</p>
   <p class=3D"note" role=3D"note">This is a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=
=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#willful-violation" id=3D"ref-for-willful=
-violation">willful violation</a> of RFC 8174, motivated by legibility and =
a desire
to preserve long-standing practice in many non-IETF-published pre-RFC 8174 =
documents. <a data-link-type=3D"biblio" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.o=
rg/#biblio-rfc8174" title=3D"Ambiguity of Uppercase vs Lowercase in RFC 211=
9 Key Words">[RFC8174]</a>

</p>
   <p>All of the above is applicable to both this standard and any document=
 that uses this standard.
Documents using this standard are encouraged to limit themselves to "must",=
 "must not", "should",
and "may", and to use these in their lowercase form as that is generally co=
nsidered to be more
readable.

</p>
   <p>For non-normative content "strongly encouraged", "strongly discourage=
d", "encouraged",
"discouraged", "can", "cannot", "could", "could not", "might", and "might n=
ot" can be used instead.


</p>
   <h3 class=3D"heading settled" data-level=3D"2.2" id=3D"other-specs"><spa=
n class=3D"secno">2.2. </span><span class=3D"content">Compliance with other=
 specifications</span><a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.wha=
twg.org/#other-specs"></a></h3>
   <p>In general, specifications interact with and rely on a wide variety o=
f other specifications. In
certain circumstances, unfortunately, conflicting needs require a specifica=
tion to violate the
requirements of other specifications. When this occurs, a document using th=
e Infra Standard should
denote such transgressions as a <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D=
"dfn" data-export=3D"" id=3D"willful-violation">willful violation</dfn>, an=
d note the reason for that
violation.

</p>
   <p class=3D"example" id=3D"example-willful-violation"><a class=3D"self-l=
ink" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-willful-violation"></a>=
The previous section, <a href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#conformance=
">=C2=A7=E2=80=AF2.1 Conformance</a>, documents a
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#willful-vi=
olation" id=3D"ref-for-willful-violation=E2=91=A0">willful violation</a> of=
 RFC 8174 committed by <cite>Infra</cite>.


</p>
   <h3 class=3D"heading settled" data-level=3D"2.3" id=3D"terminology"><spa=
n class=3D"secno">2.3. </span><span class=3D"content">Terminology</span><a =
class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#terminology"></a=
></h3>
   <p>The word "or", in cases where both inclusive "or" and exclusive "or" =
are possible (e.g., "if
either width or height is zero"), means an inclusive "or" (implying "or bot=
h"), unless it is called
out as being exclusive (with "but not both").

</p>
   <hr>
   <p>A <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" i=
d=3D"user-agent">user agent</dfn> is any software entity that acts on behal=
f
of a user, for example by retrieving and rendering web content and facilita=
ting end user interaction
with it. In specifications using the Infra Standard, the user agent is gene=
rally an instance of the client software
that implements the specification. The client software itself is known as a=
n <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" id=3D"i=
mplementation">implementation</dfn>.
A person can use many different <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://i=
nfra.spec.whatwg.org/#user-agent" id=3D"ref-for-user-agent">user agents</a>=
 in their day-to-day life, including by configuring an
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#implementa=
tion" id=3D"ref-for-implementation">implementation</a> to act as several <a=
 data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#user-agent" =
id=3D"ref-for-user-agent=E2=91=A0">user agents</a> at once, for example by =
using multiple profiles
or the implementation=E2=80=99s private browsing mode.

</p>
   <p>If something is said to be <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=
=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" id=3D"implementation-defined">implementation-defi=
ned</dfn>, the particulars of what is
said to be <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/=
#implementation-defined" id=3D"ref-for-implementation-defined">implementati=
on-defined</a> are up to the <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infr=
a.spec.whatwg.org/#implementation" id=3D"ref-for-implementation=E2=91=A0">i=
mplementation</a>. In the absence of such
language, the reverse holds: <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infr=
a.spec.whatwg.org/#implementation" id=3D"ref-for-implementation=E2=91=A1">i=
mplementations</a> have to follow the rules laid out in documents using
this standard.

</p>
   <p class=3D"example" id=3D"example-implementation-defined"><a class=3D"s=
elf-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-implementation-def=
ined"></a>Insert U+000A (LF) code points into
<var>input</var> in an <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec=
.whatwg.org/#implementation-defined" id=3D"ref-for-implementation-defined=
=E2=91=A0">implementation-defined</a> manner such that each resulting line =
has
no more than <var>width</var> code points. For the purposes of this require=
ment, lines are
delimited by the start of <var>input</var>, the end of <var>input</var>, an=
d
U+000A (LF).


</p>
   <h3 class=3D"heading settled" data-level=3D"2.4" id=3D"privacy"><span cl=
ass=3D"secno">2.4. </span><span class=3D"content">Privacy concerns</span><a=
 class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#privacy"></a></=
h3>
   <p>Some features that are defined in documents using the Infra Standard =
might trade user convenience
for a measure of user privacy.

</p>
   <p>In general, due to the internet=E2=80=99s architecture, a user can be=
 distinguished from another by the
user=E2=80=99s IP address. IP addresses do not perfectly match to a user; a=
s a user moves from device to
device, or from network to network, their IP address will change; similarly=
, NAT routing, proxy
servers, and shared computers enable packets that appear to all come from a=
 single IP address to
actually map to multiple users. Technologies such as onion routing can be u=
sed to further anonymize
requests so that requests from a single user at one node on the internet ap=
pear to come from many
disparate parts of the network. <a data-link-type=3D"biblio" href=3D"https:=
//infra.spec.whatwg.org/#biblio-rfc791" title=3D"Internet Protocol">[RFC791=
]</a>

</p>
   <p>However, the IP address used for a user=E2=80=99s requests is not the=
 only mechanism by which a user=E2=80=99s
requests could be related to each other. Cookies, for example, are designed=
 specifically to enable
this, and are the basis of most of the web=E2=80=99s session features that =
enable you to log into a site
with which you have an account. More generally, any kind of cache mechanism=
 or shared state,
including but not limited to HSTS, the HTTP cache, grouping of connections,=
 storage APIs, can and
ought to be expected to be abused. <a data-link-type=3D"biblio" href=3D"htt=
ps://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#biblio-cookies" title=3D"HTTP State Management =
Mechanism">[COOKIES]</a> <a data-link-type=3D"biblio" href=3D"https://infra=
.spec.whatwg.org/#biblio-rfc6797" title=3D"HTTP Strict Transport Security (=
HSTS)">[RFC6797]</a> <a data-link-type=3D"biblio" href=3D"https://infra.spe=
c.whatwg.org/#biblio-storage" title=3D"Storage Standard">[STORAGE]</a>

</p>
   <p>There are other mechanisms that are more subtle. Certain characterist=
ics of a user=E2=80=99s system can
be used to distinguish groups of users from each other. By collecting enoug=
h such information, an
individual user=E2=80=99s browser=E2=80=99s "digital fingerprint" can be co=
mputed, which can be better than an IP
address in ascertaining which requests are from the same user.

</p>
   <p>Grouping requests in this manner, especially across multiple sites, c=
an be used for malevolent
purposes, e.g., governments combining information such as the person=E2=80=
=99s home address (determined from
the addresses they use when getting driving directions on one site) with th=
eir apparent political
affiliations (determined by examining the forum sites that they participate=
 in) to determine whether
the person should be prevented from voting in an election.

</p>
   <p>Since the malevolent purposes can be remarkably evil, user agent impl=
ementors and specification
authors are strongly encouraged to minimize leaking information that could =
be used to fingerprint or
track a user.

</p>
   <p>Unfortunately, as the first paragraph in this section implies, someti=
mes there is great benefit
to be derived from exposing APIs that can also be abused for fingerprinting=
 and tracking purposes,
so it=E2=80=99s not as easy as blocking all possible leaks. For instance, t=
he ability to log into a site to
post under a specific identity requires that the user=E2=80=99s requests be=
 identifiable as all being from
the same user, more or less by definition. More subtly, though, information=
 such as how wide text
is, which is necessary for many effects that involve drawing text onto a ca=
nvas (e.g., any effect
that involves drawing a border around the text) also leaks information that=
 can be used to group a
user=E2=80=99s requests. (In this case, by potentially exposing, via a brut=
e force search, which fonts a
user has installed, information which can vary considerably from user to us=
er.)

</p>
   <p><a class=3D"tracking-vector" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#t=
racking-vector" style=3D"color: currentcolor"><img alt=3D"(This is a tracki=
ng vector.)" class=3D"darkmode-aware" crossorigin=3D"" height=3D"64" src=3D=
"https://resources.whatwg.org/tracking-vector.svg" title=3D"There is a trac=
king vector here." width=3D"46"></a> Features that are defined in documents=
 using the Infra Standard that can be used
as a <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" id=
=3D"tracking-vector">tracking vector</dfn> are marked as this paragraph is.

</p>
   <p>Other features in the platform can be used for the same purpose, incl=
uding, but not limited to:

</p>
   <ul>
    <li>The exact list of which features a user agents supports.
=20
    </li><li>The maximum allowed stack depth for recursion in script.
=20
    </li><li>Features that describe the user=E2=80=99s environment.
=20
    </li><li>The user=E2=80=99s time zone.
=20
    </li><li>HTTP request headers.

   </li></ul>
   <h2 class=3D"heading settled" data-level=3D"3" id=3D"algorithms"><span c=
lass=3D"secno">3. </span><span class=3D"content">Algorithms</span><a class=
=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#algorithms"></a></h2>
   <h3 class=3D"heading settled" data-level=3D"3.1" id=3D"algorithm-conform=
ance"><span class=3D"secno">3.1. </span><span class=3D"content">Conformance=
</span><a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#algori=
thm-conformance"></a></h3>
   <p>Algorithms, and requirements phrased in the imperative as part of alg=
orithms (such as "strip any
leading spaces" or "return false") are to be interpreted with the meaning o=
f the keyword (e.g.,
"must") used in introducing the algorithm or step. If no such keyword is us=
ed, must is implied.

</p>
   <div class=3D"example" id=3D"example-algorithms">
    <a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-a=
lgorithms"></a>
=20
    <p>For example, were the spec to say:</p>
    <div class=3D"exemplary-prose">
     <p class=3D"allow-2119">To <dfn data-var-ignore=3D"">eat an orange</df=
n>, the user must:

  </p>
     <ol class=3D"brief">
      <li>Peel the orange.
  =20
      </li><li>Separate each slice of the orange.
  =20
      </li><li>Eat the orange slices.
 =20
     </li></ol>
    </div>
    <p>it would be equivalent to the following:</p>
    <div class=3D"exemplary-prose">
     <p>To <dfn data-var-ignore=3D"">eat an orange</dfn>:

  </p>
     <ol class=3D"brief">
      <li class=3D"allow-2119">The user must peel the orange.
  =20
      </li><li class=3D"allow-2119">The user must separate each slice of th=
e orange.
  =20
      </li><li class=3D"allow-2119">The user must eat the orange slices.
 =20
     </li></ol>
    </div>
    <p class=3D"allow-2119">Here the key word is "must".</p>
    <p class=3D"allow-2119">Modifying the above example, if the algorithm w=
as introduced only with "To eat
 an orange:", it would still have the same meaning, as "must" is implied.
</p>
   </div>
   <p>Conformance requirements phrased as algorithms or specific steps may =
be implemented in any
manner, so long as the end result is equivalent. (In particular, the algori=
thms are intended to be
easy to follow, and not intended to be performant.)

</p>
   <p class=3D"note" role=3D"note">Performance is tricky to get correct as =
it is influenced by user perception, computer
architectures, and different types of input that can change over time in ho=
w common they are. For
instance, a JavaScript engine likely has many different code paths for what=
 is standardized as a
single algorithm, in order to optimize for speed or memory consumption. Sta=
ndardizing all those code
paths would be an insurmountable task and not productive as they would not =
stand the test of time
as well as the single algorithm would. Therefore performance is best left a=
s a field to compete
over.


</p>
   <h3 class=3D"heading settled" data-level=3D"3.2" id=3D"algorithm-limits"=
><span class=3D"secno">3.2. </span><span class=3D"content">Avoid limits on =
algorithm inputs</span><a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.wh=
atwg.org/#algorithm-limits"></a></h3>
   <p>A document using the Infra Standard generally should not enforce spec=
ific limits on algorithm
inputs with regards to their size, resource usage, or equivalent. This allo=
ws for competition among
user agents and avoids constraining the potential computing needs of the fu=
ture.

</p>
   <p><a class=3D"tracking-vector" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#t=
racking-vector" style=3D"color: currentcolor"><img alt=3D"(This is a tracki=
ng vector.)" class=3D"darkmode-aware" crossorigin=3D"" height=3D"64" src=3D=
"https://resources.whatwg.org/tracking-vector.svg" title=3D"There is a trac=
king vector here." width=3D"46"></a> Nevertheless, user agents may impose <=
a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#implementat=
ion-defined" id=3D"ref-for-implementation-defined=E2=91=A1">implementation-=
defined</a> limits on
otherwise unconstrained inputs. E.g., to prevent denial of service attacks,=
 to guard against running
out of memory, or to work around platform-specific limitations.

</p>
   <p class=3D"note" role=3D"note">Global resource limits can be used as si=
de channels through a variant on a resource
exhaustion attack, whereby the attacker can observe whether a victim applic=
ation reaches the global
limit. Limits could also be used to fingerprint the user agent, but only if=
 they make the user agent
more unique in some manner, e.g., if they are specific to the underlying ha=
rdware.

</p>
   <p class=3D"example" id=3D"example-algorithm-limits"><a class=3D"self-li=
nk" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-algorithm-limits"></a>An=
 API that allows creating an in-memory bitmap might
be specified to allow any dimensions, or any dimensions up to some large li=
mit like JavaScript=E2=80=99s
<code>Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER</code>. However, implementations can choose t=
o impose some
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#implementa=
tion-defined" id=3D"ref-for-implementation-defined=E2=91=A2">implementation=
-defined</a> (and thus not specified) limit on the dimensions, instead of
attempting to allocate huge amounts of memory.

</p>
   <p class=3D"example" id=3D"example-algorithm-limits-language"><a class=
=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-algorithm-lim=
its-language"></a>A programming language might not have a
maximum call stack size specified. However, implementations could choose to=
 impose one for practical
reasons.

</p>
   <p>As code can end up depending on a particular limit, it can be useful =
to define a limit for
interoperability. Sometimes, embracing that is not problematic for the futu=
re, and can make the code
run in more user agents.

</p>
   <p>It can also be useful to constrain an <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=
=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#implementation-defined" id=3D"ref-for-im=
plementation-defined=E2=91=A3">implementation-defined</a> limit with a lowe=
r limit.
I.e., ensuring all implementations can handle inputs of a given minimum siz=
e.


</p>
   <h3 class=3D"heading settled" data-level=3D"3.3" id=3D"algorithm-declara=
tion"><span class=3D"secno">3.3. </span><span class=3D"content">Declaration=
</span><a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#algori=
thm-declaration"></a></h3>
   <p>Algorithm names are usually verb phrases, but sometimes are given nam=
es that emphasize their
standalone existence, so that standards and readers can refer to the algori=
thm more idiomatically.

</p>
   <p class=3D"example" id=3D"example-algorithm-declaration-names"><a class=
=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-algorithm-dec=
laration-names"></a>Some algorithm names in the latter category
include "attribute change steps", "internal module script graph fetching pr=
ocedure", and "overload
resolution algorithm".

</p>
   <p>Declare algorithms by stating their name, parameters, and return type=
, in the following form:

</p>
   <p class=3D"exemplary-prose">To <dfn data-var-ignore=3D"">[algorithm nam=
e]</dfn>, given a [type1]
<var>[parameter1]</var>, a [type2] <var>[parameter2]</var>, =E2=80=A6, perf=
orm the
following steps. They return a [return type].</p>
   <p>(For non-verb phrase algorithm names, use "To perform the
<dfn data-var-ignore=3D"">[algorithm name]</dfn>=E2=80=A6". See also <a hre=
f=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#algorithm-params">=C2=A7=E2=80=AF3.4 Pa=
rameters</a> for more complicated
parameter-declaration forms.)

</p>
   <p class=3D"example" id=3D"example-algorithm-declaration"><a class=3D"se=
lf-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-algorithm-declarati=
on"></a>To <dfn data-var-ignore=3D"">parse an awesome format</dfn> given
a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte-seq=
uence" id=3D"ref-for-byte-sequence">byte sequence</a> <var>bytes</var>, per=
form the following steps. They return a
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string" id=
=3D"ref-for-string">string</a> or null.

</p>
   <p>Algorithms which do not return a value use a shorter form. This same =
shorter form can be used even
for algorithms that do return a value if the return type is relatively easy=
 to infer from the
algorithm steps:

</p>
   <p class=3D"exemplary-prose">To <dfn data-var-ignore=3D"">[algorithm nam=
e]</dfn>, given a [type1]
<var>[parameter1]</var>, a [type2] <var>[parameter2]</var>, =E2=80=A6:</p>
   <p class=3D"example" id=3D"example-algorithm-declaration-no-return"><a c=
lass=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-algorithm=
-declaration-no-return"></a>To
<dfn data-var-ignore=3D"">parse an awesome format</dfn> given a <a data-lin=
k-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte-sequence" id=3D"=
ref-for-byte-sequence=E2=91=A0">byte sequence</a> <var>bytes</var>:

</p>
   <p>Very short algorithms can be declared and specified using a single se=
ntence:

</p>
   <p class=3D"example" id=3D"example-algorithm-declaration-short"><a class=
=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-algorithm-dec=
laration-short"></a>To <dfn data-var-ignore=3D"">parse an awesome format</d=
fn>
given a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#by=
te-sequence" id=3D"ref-for-byte-sequence=E2=91=A1">byte sequence</a> <var>b=
ytes</var>, return the result of
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-uppe=
rcase" id=3D"ref-for-ascii-uppercase">ASCII uppercasing</a> the <a data-lin=
k-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#isomorphic-decode" id=
=3D"ref-for-isomorphic-decode">isomorphic decoding</a>
of <var>bytes</var>.

</p>
   <p>Types should be included in algorithm declarations, but may be omitte=
d if the parameter name is
clear enough, or if they are otherwise clear from context. (For example, be=
cause the algorithm is
a simple wrapper around another one.)

</p>
   <p class=3D"example" id=3D"example-algorithm-declaration-no-types"><a cl=
ass=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-algorithm-=
declaration-no-types"></a>To
<dfn data-var-ignore=3D"">load a classic script</dfn> given <var>url</var>,=
 return the result of performing the
internal script-loading algorithm given <var>url</var> and "<code>classic</=
code>".


</p>
   <h3 class=3D"heading settled" data-level=3D"3.4" id=3D"algorithm-params"=
><span class=3D"secno">3.4. </span><span class=3D"content">Parameters</span=
><a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#algorithm-pa=
rams"></a></h3>
   <p>Algorithm parameters are usually listed sequentially, in the fashion =
described in
<a href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#algorithm-declaration">=C2=A7=E2=
=80=AF3.3 Declaration</a>. However, there are some more complicated cases.<=
/p>
   <p>Algorithm parameters can be optional, in which case the algorithm dec=
laration must list them as
such, and list them after any non-optional parameters. They can either be g=
iven a default value, or
the algorithm body can check whether or not the argument was given. Concret=
ely, use the following
forms:

</p>
   <p class=3D"exemplary-prose">=E2=80=A6 an optional [type] <var>[paramete=
r]</var> =E2=80=A6

</p>
   <p class=3D"exemplary-prose">=E2=80=A6 an optional [type] <var>[paramete=
r]</var> (default [default
value]) =E2=80=A6

</p>
   <p>Optional <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.=
org/#boolean" id=3D"ref-for-boolean">boolean</a> parameters must have a def=
ault value specified, and that default must be
false.

</p>
   <div class=3D"example" id=3D"example-algorithm-optional-positional-param=
s">
    <a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-a=
lgorithm-optional-positional-params"></a>
=20
    <p class=3D"allow-2119">To <dfn data-var-ignore=3D"" id=3D"example-navi=
gate-algo-positional">navigate</dfn> to a
 resource <var>resource</var>, with an optional string <var>navigationType<=
/var> and
 an optional boolean <var>exceptionsEnabled</var> (default false):

 </p>
    <ol class=3D"brief">
     <li>=E2=80=A6
 =20
     </li><li>If <var>navigationType</var> was given, then do something wit=
h
  <var>navigationType</var>.
 =20
     </li><li>=E2=80=A6
=20
    </li></ol>
   </div>
   <p>To call algorithms with such optional positional parameters, the opti=
onal argument values can be
omitted, but only the trailing ones.

</p>
   <div class=3D"example" id=3D"example-algorithm-optional-positional-param=
s-calling">
    <a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-a=
lgorithm-optional-positional-params-calling"></a>
=20
    <p>Call sites to the previous example=E2=80=99s algorithm would look li=
ke one of:

 </p>
    <ul class=3D"brief">
     <li><a href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-navigate-algo-po=
sitional">Navigate</a> to <var>resource</var>.

 =20
     </li><li><a href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-navigate-al=
go-positional">Navigate</a> to <var>resource</var> with
  "<code>form submission</code>".

 =20
     </li><li><a href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-navigate-al=
go-positional">Navigate</a> to <var>resource</var> with
  "<code>form submission</code>" and true.
=20
    </li></ul>
    <p>But, there would be no way to supply a non-default value for the thi=
rd
 (<var>exceptionsEnabled</var>) argument, while leaving the second
 (<var>navigationType</var>) argument as not-given. Additionally, the last =
of these calls is
 fairly unclear for readers, as the fact that "true" means "exceptions enab=
led" requires going back
 to the algorithm=E2=80=99s declaration and counting parameters. Read on fo=
r how to fix these issues!
</p>
   </div>
   <p>Optional named parameters, instead of positional ones, can be used to=
 increase clarity and
flexibility at the call site. Such parameters are marked up as both variabl=
es and definitions, and
linked to from their call sites.

</p>
   <div class=3D"example" id=3D"example-algorithm-optional-named-params">
    <a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-a=
lgorithm-optional-named-params"></a>
=20
    <p class=3D"allow-2119">To <dfn data-var-ignore=3D"" id=3D"example-navi=
gate-algo-named">navigate</dfn> to a
 resource <var>resource</var>, with an optional string
 <dfn data-var-ignore=3D"" id=3D"example-navigate-algo-navigationType"><var=
>navigationType</var></dfn> and an
 optional boolean
 <dfn data-var-ignore=3D"" id=3D"example-navigate-algo-exceptionsEnabled"><=
var>exceptionsEnabled</var></dfn>
 (default false):

 </p>
    <ol class=3D"brief">
     <li>=E2=80=A6
 =20
     </li><li>If <var>navigationType</var> was given, then do something wit=
h
  <var>navigationType</var>.
 =20
     </li><li>=E2=80=A6
=20
    </li></ol>
    <p>Call sites would then look like one of:

 </p>
    <ul class=3D"brief">
     <li><a href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-navigate-algo-na=
med">Navigate</a> to <var>resource</var>.

 =20
     </li><li><a href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-navigate-al=
go-named">Navigate</a> to <var>resource</var> with
  <a href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-navigate-algo-navigatio=
nType"><i>navigationType</i></a> set to
  "<code>form-submission</code>".

 =20
     </li><li><a href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-navigate-al=
go-named">Navigate</a> to <var>resource</var> with
  <a href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-navigate-algo-exception=
sEnabled"><i>exceptionsEnabled</i></a> set to true.

 =20
     </li><li><a href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-navigate-al=
go-named">Navigate</a> to <var>resource</var> with
  <a href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-navigate-algo-navigatio=
nType"><i>navigationType</i></a> set to
  "<code>form-submission</code>" and
  <a href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-navigate-algo-exception=
sEnabled"><i>exceptionsEnabled</i></a> set to
  true.
=20
    </li></ul>
   </div>
   <p class=3D"note" role=3D"note">Note how within the algorithm steps, the=
 argument value is not linked to the parameter
declaration; it remains just a variable reference. Linking to the parameter=
 declaration is done only
at the call sites.

</p>
   <p>Non-optional named parameters may also be used, using the same conven=
tion of marking them up as
both variables and definitions, and linking to them from call sites. This c=
an improve clarity at the
call sites.

</p>
   <p class=3D"allow-2119 example" id=3D"example-algorithm-non-optional-nam=
ed-params"><a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#ex=
ample-algorithm-non-optional-named-params"></a><a data-link-type=3D"dfn" hr=
ef=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#boolean" id=3D"ref-for-boolean=E2=91=
=A0">Boolean</a>
parameters are a case where naming the parameter can be significantly clear=
er than leaving it as
positional, regardless of optionality. See
<a href=3D"https://ariya.io/2011/08/hall-of-api-shame-boolean-trap"><cite>T=
he Pitfalls of Boolean
Trap</cite></a> for discussion of this in the context of programming langua=
ges.

</p>
   <p>Another complementary technique for improving clarity is to package u=
p related values into a
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#struct" id=
=3D"ref-for-struct">struct</a>, and pass that struct as a parameter. This i=
s especially applicable when the same set
of related values is used as the input to multiple algorithms.


</p>
   <h3 class=3D"heading settled" data-level=3D"3.5" id=3D"variables"><span =
class=3D"secno">3.5. </span><span class=3D"content">Variables</span><a clas=
s=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#variables"></a></h3>
   <p>A variable is declared with "let" and changed with "set".

</p>
   <p class=3D"example" id=3D"example-variable"><a class=3D"self-link" href=
=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-variable"></a>Let <var>list</var=
> be a new <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/=
#list" id=3D"ref-for-list">list</a>.</p>
   <div class=3D"example" id=3D"example-variable-null">
    <a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-v=
ariable-null"></a>
=20
    <ol>
     <li>
      <p>Let <var>value</var> be null.

  </p>
     </li><li>
      <p>If <var>input</var> is a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https:/=
/infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A0">string</a>, =
then set <var>value</var> to <var>input</var>.

  </p>
     </li><li>
      <p>Otherwise, set <var>value</var> to <var>input</var>, <a data-link-=
type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://encoding.spec.whatwg.org/#utf-8-decode" id=3D"=
ref-for-utf-8-decode">UTF-8 decoded</a>.

  </p>
     </li><li>
      <p><a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#a=
ssert" id=3D"ref-for-assert">Assert</a>: <var>value</var> is a <a data-link=
-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string" id=3D"ref-for-=
string=E2=91=A1">string</a>.
 </p>
    </li></ol>
   </div>
   <p class=3D"example" id=3D"example-variable-ternary"><a class=3D"self-li=
nk" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-variable-ternary"></a>Le=
t <var>activationTarget</var> be
<var>target</var> if <var>isActivationEvent</var> is true and <var>target</=
var> has activation
behavior; otherwise null.

</p>
   <p>Variables must not be used before they are declared. Variables are
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scope_(computer_science)#Block_sco=
pe">block scoped</a>.
Variables must not be declared more than once per algorithm.

</p>
   <p>A multiple assignment syntax can be used to assign multiple variables=
 to the <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#tu=
ple" id=3D"ref-for-tuple">tuple</a>=E2=80=99s
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#struct-ite=
m" id=3D"ref-for-struct-item">items</a>, by surrounding the variable names =
with U+0028 LEFT PARENTHESIS and
U+0029 RIGHT PARENTHESIS, and separating each variable name with U+002C (,)=
. The number of variables
assigned cannot differ from the number of <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D=
"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#struct-item" id=3D"ref-for-struct-item=E2=
=91=A0">items</a> in the <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.sp=
ec.whatwg.org/#tuple" id=3D"ref-for-tuple=E2=91=A0">tuple</a>.

</p>
   <div class=3D"example" id=3D"example-tuple-multiple-assignment">
    <a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-t=
uple-multiple-assignment"></a>
=20
    <ol>
     <li>
      <p>Let <var>statusInstance</var> be the status (200, `<code>OK</code>=
`).
  </p>
     </li><li>
      <p>Let (<var>status</var>, <var>statusMessage</var>) be <var>statusIn=
stance</var>.
 </p>
    </li></ol>
    <p>Assigning <var>status</var> and <var>statusMessage</var> could be wr=
itten as two separate steps that use an index
 or <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#struct=
-name" id=3D"ref-for-struct-name">name</a> to access the <a data-link-type=
=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#tuple" id=3D"ref-for-tuple=
=E2=91=A1">tuple</a>=E2=80=99s <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://in=
fra.spec.whatwg.org/#struct-item" id=3D"ref-for-struct-item=E2=91=A1">items=
</a>.
</p>
   </div>
   <h3 class=3D"heading settled" data-level=3D"3.6" id=3D"algorithm-control=
-flow"><span class=3D"secno">3.6. </span><span class=3D"content">Control fl=
ow</span><a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#algo=
rithm-control-flow"></a></h3>
   <p>The control flow of algorithms is such that a requirement to "return"=
 or "throw" terminates the
algorithm the statement was in. "Return" will hand the given value, if any,=
 to its caller. "Throw"
will make the caller automatically rethrow the given value, if any, and the=
reby terminate the
caller=E2=80=99s algorithm. Using prose the caller has the ability to "catc=
h" the exception and perform
another action.


</p>
   <h3 class=3D"heading settled" data-level=3D"3.7" id=3D"algorithm-conditi=
onal-abort"><span class=3D"secno">3.7. </span><span class=3D"content">Condi=
tional abort</span><a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg=
.org/#algorithm-conditional-abort"></a></h3>
   <p>Sometimes it is useful to stop performing a series of steps once a co=
ndition becomes true.

</p>
   <p>To do this, state that a given series of steps will <dfn class=3D"dfn=
-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" id=3D"abort-when">abort wh=
en</dfn> a specific
<var>condition</var> is reached. This indicates that the specified steps mu=
st be evaluated, not
as-written, but by additionally inserting a step before each of them that e=
valuates
<var>condition</var>, and if <var>condition</var> evaluates to true, skips =
the remaining steps.

</p>
   <p>In such algorithms, the subsequent step can be annotated to run <dfn =
class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" id=3D"if-abort=
ed">if aborted</dfn>, in
which case it must run if any of the preceding steps were skipped due to th=
e <var>condition</var>
of the preceding <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatw=
g.org/#abort-when" id=3D"ref-for-abort-when">abort when</a> step evaluated =
to true.

</p>
   <div class=3D"example" id=3D"example-conditional-abort">
    <a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-c=
onditional-abort"></a>
=20
    <p>The following algorithm

 </p>
    <ol>
     <li>
      <p>Let <var>result</var> be an empty <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=
=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#list" id=3D"ref-for-list=E2=91=A0">list<=
/a>.

  </p>
     </li><li>
      <p>Run these steps, but <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://inf=
ra.spec.whatwg.org/#abort-when" id=3D"ref-for-abort-when=E2=91=A0">abort wh=
en</a> the user clicks the "Cancel" button:

   </p>
      <ol>
       <li>
        <p>Compute the first million digits of <var>=CF=80</var>, and <a da=
ta-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#list-append" id=
=3D"ref-for-list-append">append</a> the result
    to <var>result</var>.

    </p>
       </li><li>
        <p>Compute the first million digits of <var>e</var>, and <a data-li=
nk-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#list-append" id=3D"r=
ef-for-list-append=E2=91=A0">append</a> the result to
    <var>result</var>.

    </p>
       </li><li>
        <p>Compute the first million digits of <var>=CF=86</var>, and <a da=
ta-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#list-append" id=
=3D"ref-for-list-append=E2=91=A1">append</a> the result
    to <var>result</var>.
   </p>
      </li></ol>
     </li><li>
      <p><a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#i=
f-aborted" id=3D"ref-for-if-aborted">If aborted</a>, <a data-link-type=3D"d=
fn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#list-append" id=3D"ref-for-list-=
append=E2=91=A2">append</a> "<code>Didn=E2=80=99t finish!</code>" to <var>r=
esult</var>.
 </p>
    </li></ol>
    <p>is equivalent to the more verbose formulation</p>
    <ol>
     <li>
      <p>Let <var>result</var> be an empty <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=
=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#list" id=3D"ref-for-list=E2=91=A1">list<=
/a>.

  </p>
     </li><li>
      <p>If the user has not clicked the "Cancel" button:

   </p>
      <ol>
       <li>
        <p>Compute the first million digits of <var>=CF=80</var>, and <a da=
ta-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#list-append" id=
=3D"ref-for-list-append=E2=91=A3">append</a> the result
    to <var>result</var>.

    </p>
       </li><li>
        <p>If the user has not clicked the "Cancel" button:

     </p>
        <ol>
         <li>
          <p>Compute the first million digits of <var>e</var>, and <a data-=
link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#list-append" id=3D=
"ref-for-list-append=E2=91=A4">append</a> the result to
      <var>result</var>.

      </p>
         </li><li>
          <p>If the user has not clicked the "Cancel" button, then compute =
the first million digits
      of <var>=CF=86</var>, and <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://i=
nfra.spec.whatwg.org/#list-append" id=3D"ref-for-list-append=E2=91=A5">appe=
nd</a> the result to <var>result</var>.
     </p>
        </li></ol>
      </li></ol>
     </li><li>
      <p>If the user clicked the "Cancel" button, then <a data-link-type=3D=
"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#list-append" id=3D"ref-for-lis=
t-append=E2=91=A6">append</a>
  "<code>Didn=E2=80=99t finish!</code>" to <var>result</var>.
 </p>
    </li></ol>
   </div>
   <p class=3D"note" role=3D"note">Whenever this construct is used, impleme=
ntations are allowed to evaluate
<var>condition</var> during the specified steps rather than before and afte=
r each step, as long as
the end result is indistinguishable. For instance, as long as <var>result</=
var> in the above example is not
mutated during a compute operation, the user agent could stop the computati=
on.


</p>
   <h3 class=3D"heading settled" data-level=3D"3.8" id=3D"algorithm-conditi=
onal-statements"><span class=3D"secno">3.8. </span><span class=3D"content">=
Conditional statements</span><a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.s=
pec.whatwg.org/#algorithm-conditional-statements"></a></h3>
   <p>Algorithms with conditional statements should use the keywords "if", =
"then", and "otherwise".

</p>
   <div class=3D"example" id=3D"example-conditional-statement">
    <a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-c=
onditional-statement"></a>
=20
    <ol>
     <li>
      <p>Let <var>value</var> be null.

  </p>
     </li><li>
      <p>If <var>input</var> is a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https:/=
/infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A2">string</a>, =
then set <var>value</var> to <var>input</var>.

  </p>
     </li><li>
      <p>Return <var>value</var>.
 </p>
    </li></ol>
   </div>
   <p>Once the keyword "otherwise" is used, the keyword "then" is omitted.<=
/p>
   <div class=3D"example" id=3D"example-conditional-statement-no-then-1">
    <a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-c=
onditional-statement-no-then-1"></a>
=20
    <ol>
     <li>
      <p>Let <var>value</var> be null.

  </p>
     </li><li>
      <p>If <var>input</var> is a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https:/=
/infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A3">string</a>, =
then set <var>value</var> to <var>input</var>.

  </p>
     </li><li>
      <p>Otherwise, set <var>value</var> to failure.

  </p>
     </li><li>
      <p>Return <var>value</var>.
 </p>
    </li></ol>
   </div>
   <div class=3D"example" id=3D"example-conditional-statement-no-then-2">
    <a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-c=
onditional-statement-no-then-2"></a>
=20
    <ol>
     <li>
      <p>Let <var>value</var> be null.

  </p>
     </li><li>
      <p>If <var>input</var> is a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https:/=
/infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A4">string</a>, =
then set <var>value</var> to <var>input</var>.

  </p>
     </li><li>
      <p>Otherwise, if <var>input</var> is a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=
=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#list" id=3D"ref-for-list=E2=91=A2">list<=
/a> of <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#str=
ing" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A5">strings</a>, set <var>value</var> to <v=
ar>input</var>[0].

  </p>
     </li><li>
      <p>Otherwise, throw a <code class=3D"idl"><a data-link-type=3D"idl" h=
ref=3D"https://webidl.spec.whatwg.org/#exceptiondef-typeerror" id=3D"ref-fo=
r-exceptiondef-typeerror">TypeError</a></code>.

  </p>
     </li><li>
      <p>Return <var>value</var>.
 </p>
    </li></ol>
   </div>
   <h3 class=3D"heading settled" data-level=3D"3.9" id=3D"algorithm-iterati=
on"><span class=3D"secno">3.9. </span><span class=3D"content">Iteration</sp=
an><a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#algorithm-=
iteration"></a></h3>
   <p>There=E2=80=99s a variety of ways to repeat a set of steps until a co=
ndition is reached.

</p>
   <p class=3D"note" role=3D"note">The Infra Standard is not (yet) exhausti=
ve on this; please file an issue if you need
something.

</p>
   <dl>
    <dt>For each
=20
    </dt><dd>
     <p>As defined for <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec=
.whatwg.org/#list-iterate" id=3D"ref-for-list-iterate">lists</a> (and deriv=
atives) and
 <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#map-itera=
te" id=3D"ref-for-map-iterate">maps</a>.

 </p>
    </dd><dt><dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-for=3D"iteration" data-dfn=
-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" data-lt=3D"while" id=3D"iteration-while">Whi=
le</dfn>
=20
    </dt><dd>
     <p>An instruction to repeat a set of steps as long as a condition is m=
et.

  </p>
     <div class=3D"example" id=3D"example-while">
      <a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example=
-while"></a>
  =20
      <p>While <var>condition</var> is "<code>met</code>":

   </p>
      <ol>
       <li>
        <p>=E2=80=A6
   </p>
      </li></ol>
     </div>
   </dd></dl>
   <p>An iteration=E2=80=99s flow can be controlled via requirements to
<dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-for=3D"iteration" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn"=
 data-export=3D"" id=3D"iteration-continue">continue</dfn> or <dfn class=3D=
"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-for=3D"iteration" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=
=3D"" id=3D"iteration-break">break</dfn>.
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#iteration-=
continue" id=3D"ref-for-iteration-continue">Continue</a> will skip over any=
 remaining steps in an iteration, proceeding to the
next item. If no further items remain, the iteration will stop. <a data-lin=
k-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#iteration-break" id=
=3D"ref-for-iteration-break">Break</a> will skip
over any remaining steps in an iteration, and skip over any remaining items=
 as well, stopping the
iteration.

</p>
   <div class=3D"example" id=3D"example-break-continue">
    <a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-b=
reak-continue"></a>
=20
    <p>Let <var>example</var> be the <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"http=
s://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#list" id=3D"ref-for-list=E2=91=A3">list</a> =C2=
=AB 1, 2, 3, 4 =C2=BB. The following prose would perform <var>operation</va=
r>
 upon 1, then 2, then 3, then 4:

 </p>
    <ol>
     <li>
      <p><a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#l=
ist-iterate" id=3D"ref-for-list-iterate=E2=91=A0">For each</a> <var>item</v=
ar> of <var>example</var>:
   </p>
      <ol>
       <li>Perform <var>operation</var> on <var>item</var>.
  =20
      </li></ol>
    </li></ol>
    <p>The following prose would perform <var>operation</var> upon 1, then =
2, then 4. 3 would be skipped.

 </p>
    <ol>
     <li>
      <p><a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#l=
ist-iterate" id=3D"ref-for-list-iterate=E2=91=A1">For each</a> <var>item</v=
ar> of <var>example</var>:
   </p>
      <ol>
       <li>If <var>item</var> is 3, then <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"=
https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#iteration-continue" id=3D"ref-for-iteration-=
continue=E2=91=A0">continue</a>.
   =20
       </li><li>Perform <var>operation</var> on <var>item</var>.
  =20
      </li></ol>
    </li></ol>
    <p>The following prose would perform <var>operation</var> upon 1, then =
2. 3 and 4 would be skipped.

 </p>
    <ol>
     <li>
      <p><a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#l=
ist-iterate" id=3D"ref-for-list-iterate=E2=91=A2">For each</a> <var>item</v=
ar> of <var>example</var>:
   </p>
      <ol>
       <li>If <var>item</var> is 3, then <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"=
https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#iteration-break" id=3D"ref-for-iteration-bre=
ak=E2=91=A0">break</a>.
   =20
       </li><li>Perform <var>operation</var> on <var>item</var>.
  =20
      </li></ol>
    </li></ol>
   </div>
   <h3 class=3D"heading settled" data-level=3D"3.10" id=3D"assertions"><spa=
n class=3D"secno">3.10. </span><span class=3D"content">Assertions</span><a =
class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#assertions"></a>=
</h3>
   <p>To improve readability, it can sometimes help to add assertions to al=
gorithms, stating
invariants. To do this, write "<dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"=
dfn" data-export=3D"" id=3D"assert">Assert</dfn>:", followed by a statement=
 that must be
true. If the statement ends up being false that indicates an issue with the=
 document using the Infra
Standard that should be reported and addressed.

</p>
   <p class=3D"note" role=3D"note">Since the statement can only ever be tru=
e, it has no implications for implementations.

</p>
   <div class=3D"example" id=3D"example-assert">
    <a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-a=
ssert"></a>
=20
    <ol>
     <li>
      <p>Let <var>x</var> be "<code>Aperture Science</code>".
  </p>
     </li><li>
      <p><a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#a=
ssert" id=3D"ref-for-assert=E2=91=A0">Assert</a>: <var>x</var> is "<code>Ap=
erture Science</code>".
 </p>
    </li></ol>
   </div>
   <h2 class=3D"heading settled" data-level=3D"4" id=3D"primitive-data-type=
s"><span class=3D"secno">4. </span><span class=3D"content">Primitive data t=
ypes</span><a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#pr=
imitive-data-types"></a></h2>
   <h3 class=3D"heading settled" data-level=3D"4.1" id=3D"nulls"><span clas=
s=3D"secno">4.1. </span><span class=3D"content">Nulls</span><a class=3D"sel=
f-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#nulls"></a></h3>
   <p>The value null is used to indicate the lack of a value. It can be use=
d interchangeably with the
JavaScript <b>null</b> value. <a data-link-type=3D"biblio" href=3D"https://=
infra.spec.whatwg.org/#biblio-ecma-262" title=3D"ECMAScript Language Specif=
ication">[ECMA-262]</a>

</p>
   <p class=3D"example" id=3D"example-null"><a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"=
https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-null"></a>Let <var>element</var> be =
null.

</p>
   <p class=3D"example" id=3D"example-null-return"><a class=3D"self-link" h=
ref=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-null-return"></a>If <var>inpu=
t</var> is the empty string, then return null.


</p>
   <h3 class=3D"heading settled" data-level=3D"4.2" id=3D"booleans"><span c=
lass=3D"secno">4.2. </span><span class=3D"content">Booleans</span><a class=
=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#booleans"></a></h3>
   <p>A <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" i=
d=3D"boolean">boolean</dfn> is either true or false.

</p>
   <p class=3D"example" id=3D"example-boolean"><a class=3D"self-link" href=
=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-boolean"></a>Let <var>elementSee=
n</var> be false.


</p>
   <h3 class=3D"heading settled" data-level=3D"4.3" id=3D"numbers"><span cl=
ass=3D"secno">4.3. </span><span class=3D"content">Numbers</span><a class=3D=
"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#numbers"></a></h3>
   <p class=3D"XXX">Numbers are complicated; please see
<a href=3D"https://github.com/whatwg/infra/issues/87">issue #87</a>. In due=
 course we hope to offer
more guidance here around types and mathematical operations. Help appreciat=
ed!

</p>
   <hr>
   <p>An <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" =
id=3D"8-bit-unsigned-integer">8-bit unsigned integer</dfn> is an integer in=
 the range 0 to 255 (0 to
2<sup>8</sup> =E2=88=92 1), inclusive.

</p>
   <p>A <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" i=
d=3D"16-bit-unsigned-integer">16-bit unsigned integer</dfn> is an integer i=
n the range 0 to 65535 (0 to
2<sup>16</sup> =E2=88=92 1), inclusive.

</p>
   <p>A <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" i=
d=3D"32-bit-unsigned-integer">32-bit unsigned integer</dfn> is an integer i=
n the range 0 to 4294967295 (0 to
2<sup>32</sup> =E2=88=92 1), inclusive.

</p>
   <p>A <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" i=
d=3D"64-bit-unsigned-integer">64-bit unsigned integer</dfn> is an integer i=
n the range 0 to 18446744073709551615
(0 to 2<sup>64</sup> =E2=88=92 1), inclusive.

</p>
   <p>A <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" i=
d=3D"128-bit-unsigned-integer">128-bit unsigned integer</dfn> is an integer=
 in the range 0 to
340282366920938463463374607431768211455 (0 to 2<sup>128</sup> =E2=88=92 1),=
 inclusive.

</p>
   <p class=3D"example" id=3D"example-128-bit-unsigned-integer"><a class=3D=
"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-128-bit-unsigned=
-integer"></a>An IPv6 address is an
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#128-bit-un=
signed-integer" id=3D"ref-for-128-bit-unsigned-integer">128-bit unsigned in=
teger</a>.

</p>
   <hr>
   <p>An <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" =
id=3D"8-bit-signed-integer">8-bit signed integer</dfn> is an integer in the=
 range =E2=88=92128 to 127
(=E2=88=922<sup>7</sup> to 2<sup>7</sup> =E2=88=92 1), inclusive.

</p>
   <p>A <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" i=
d=3D"16-bit-signed-integer">16-bit signed integer</dfn> is an integer in th=
e range =E2=88=9232768 to 32767
(=E2=88=922<sup>15</sup> to 2<sup>15</sup> =E2=88=92 1), inclusive.

</p>
   <p>A <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" i=
d=3D"32-bit-signed-integer">32-bit signed integer</dfn> is an integer in th=
e range =E2=88=922147483648 to
2147483647 (=E2=88=922<sup>31</sup> to 2<sup>31</sup> =E2=88=92 1), inclusi=
ve.

</p>
   <p>A <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" i=
d=3D"64-bit-signed-integer">64-bit signed integer</dfn> is an integer in th=
e range =E2=88=929223372036854775808
to 9223372036854775807 (=E2=88=922<sup>63</sup> to 2<sup>63</sup> =E2=88=92=
 1), inclusive.


</p>
   <h3 class=3D"heading settled" data-level=3D"4.4" id=3D"bytes"><span clas=
s=3D"secno">4.4. </span><span class=3D"content">Bytes</span><a class=3D"sel=
f-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#bytes"></a></h3>
   <p>A <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" i=
d=3D"byte">byte</dfn> is a sequence of eight bits and is represented as "<c=
ode>0x</code>"
followed by two <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg=
.org/#ascii-upper-hex-digit" id=3D"ref-for-ascii-upper-hex-digit">ASCII upp=
er hex digits</a>, in the range 0x00 to 0xFF, inclusive. A <a data-link-typ=
e=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte" id=3D"ref-for-byte">=
byte</a>=E2=80=99s
<dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-for=3D"byte" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data=
-export=3D"" id=3D"byte-value">value</dfn> is its underlying number.

</p>
   <p class=3D"example" id=3D"example-byte-value"><a class=3D"self-link" hr=
ef=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-byte-value"></a>0x40 is a <a d=
ata-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte" id=3D"re=
f-for-byte=E2=91=A0">byte</a> whose <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https=
://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte-value" id=3D"ref-for-byte-value">value</a> i=
s 64.

</p>
   <p>An <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" =
id=3D"ascii-byte">ASCII byte</dfn> is a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"h=
ttps://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte" id=3D"ref-for-byte=E2=91=A1">byte</a> i=
n the range 0x00 (NUL) to 0x7F (DEL),
inclusive. As illustrated, an <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://inf=
ra.spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-byte" id=3D"ref-for-ascii-byte">ASCII byte</a>, e=
xcluding 0x28 and 0x29, may be followed by the
representation outlined in the <a href=3D"https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc20=
#section-2">Standard Code</a>
section of <cite>ASCII format for Network Interchange</cite>, between paren=
theses. <a data-link-type=3D"biblio" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/=
#biblio-rfc20" title=3D"ASCII format for network interchange">[RFC20]</a>

</p>
   <p>0x28 may be followed by "(left parenthesis)" and 0x29 by "(right pare=
nthesis)".

</p>
   <p class=3D"example" id=3D"example-byte-notation"><a class=3D"self-link"=
 href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-byte-notation"></a>0x49 (I)=
 when <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://encoding.spec.whatwg.org/#u=
tf-8-decode" id=3D"ref-for-utf-8-decode=E2=91=A0">UTF-8 decoded</a> becomes=
 the
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-point=
" id=3D"ref-for-code-point">code point</a> U+0049 (I).


</p>
   <h3 class=3D"heading settled" data-level=3D"4.5" id=3D"byte-sequences"><=
span class=3D"secno">4.5. </span><span class=3D"content">Byte sequences</sp=
an><a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte-seque=
nces"></a></h3>
   <p>A <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" i=
d=3D"byte-sequence">byte sequence</dfn> is a sequence of <a data-link-type=
=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte" id=3D"ref-for-byte=E2=
=91=A2">bytes</a>, represented as a space-separated
sequence of bytes. Byte sequences with bytes in the range 0x20 (SP) to 0x7E=
 (~), inclusive, can
alternately be written as a string, but using backticks instead of quotatio=
n marks, to avoid
confusion with an actual <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.sp=
ec.whatwg.org/#string" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A6">string</a>.

</p>
   <div class=3D"example" id=3D"example-byte-sequence-notation">
    <a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-b=
yte-sequence-notation"></a>
=20
    <p>0x48 0x49 can also be represented as `<code>HI</code>`.

 </p>
    <p>Headers, such as `<code>Content-Type</code>`, are <a data-link-type=
=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte-sequence" id=3D"ref-fo=
r-byte-sequence=E2=91=A2">byte sequences</a>.
</p>
   </div>
   <p class=3D"note" role=3D"note">To get a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=
=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte-sequence" id=3D"ref-for-byte-sequen=
ce=E2=91=A3">byte sequence</a> out of a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"h=
ttps://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A7">string=
</a>, using <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://encoding.spec.whatwg.=
org/#utf-8-encode" id=3D"ref-for-utf-8-encode">UTF-8 encode</a> from
<cite>Encoding</cite> is encouraged. In rare circumstances <a data-link-typ=
e=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#isomorphic-encode" id=3D"r=
ef-for-isomorphic-encode">isomorphic encode</a> might be needed.
<a data-link-type=3D"biblio" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#biblio-=
encoding" title=3D"Encoding Standard">[ENCODING]</a>

</p>
   <p>A <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#by=
te-sequence" id=3D"ref-for-byte-sequence=E2=91=A4">byte sequence</a>=E2=80=
=99s <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-for=3D"byte sequence" data-dfn-typ=
e=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" id=3D"byte-sequence-length">length</dfn> is the =
number of
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte" id=
=3D"ref-for-byte=E2=91=A3">bytes</a> it contains.

</p>
   <p>To <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" =
id=3D"byte-lowercase">byte-lowercase</dfn> a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=
=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte-sequence" id=3D"ref-for-byte-sequen=
ce=E2=91=A5">byte sequence</a>, increase each <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" hre=
f=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte" id=3D"ref-for-byte=E2=91=A4">byte=
</a> it
contains, in the range 0x41 (A) to 0x5A (Z), inclusive, by 0x20.

</p>
   <p>To <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" =
id=3D"byte-uppercase">byte-uppercase</dfn> a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=
=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte-sequence" id=3D"ref-for-byte-sequen=
ce=E2=91=A6">byte sequence</a>, subtract each <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" hre=
f=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte" id=3D"ref-for-byte=E2=91=A5">byte=
</a> it
contains, in the range 0x61 (a) to 0x7A (z), inclusive, by 0x20.

</p>
   <p>A <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#by=
te-sequence" id=3D"ref-for-byte-sequence=E2=91=A7">byte sequence</a> <var>A=
</var> is a <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D=
"" id=3D"byte-case-insensitive">byte-case-insensitive</dfn> match for a
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte-seque=
nce" id=3D"ref-for-byte-sequence=E2=91=A8">byte sequence</a> <var>B</var>, =
if the <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byt=
e-lowercase" id=3D"ref-for-byte-lowercase">byte-lowercase</a> of <var>A</va=
r> is the
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte-lower=
case" id=3D"ref-for-byte-lowercase=E2=91=A0">byte-lowercase</a> of <var>B</=
var>.

</p>
   <hr>
   <p>A <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#by=
te-sequence" id=3D"ref-for-byte-sequence=E2=91=A0=E2=93=AA">byte sequence</=
a> <var>potentialPrefix</var> is a
<dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-for=3D"byte sequence" data-dfn-type=3D"=
dfn" data-export=3D"" id=3D"byte-sequence-prefix">prefix</dfn> of a <a data=
-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte-sequence" id=
=3D"ref-for-byte-sequence=E2=91=A0=E2=91=A0">byte sequence</a> <var>input</=
var> if the
following steps return true:

</p>
   <ol>
    <li>
     <p>Let <var>i</var> be 0.

 </p>
    </li><li>
     <p><a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#it=
eration-while" id=3D"ref-for-iteration-while">While</a> true:

  </p>
     <ol>
      <li>
       <p>If <var>i</var> is greater than or equal to <var>potentialPrefix<=
/var>=E2=80=99s
   <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte-se=
quence-length" id=3D"ref-for-byte-sequence-length">length</a>, then return =
true.

   </p>
      </li><li>
       <p>If <var>i</var> is greater than or equal to <var>input</var>=E2=
=80=99s
   <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte-se=
quence-length" id=3D"ref-for-byte-sequence-length=E2=91=A0">length</a>, the=
n return false.

   </p>
      </li><li>
       <p>Let <var>potentialPrefixByte</var> be the <var>i</var>th <a data-=
link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte" id=3D"ref-fo=
r-byte=E2=91=A6">byte</a> of
   <var>potentialPrefix</var>.

   </p>
      </li><li>
       <p>Let <var>inputByte</var> be the <var>i</var>th <a data-link-type=
=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte" id=3D"ref-for-byte=E2=
=91=A7">byte</a> of <var>input</var>.

   </p>
      </li><li>
       <p>Return false if <var>potentialPrefixByte</var> is not <var>inputB=
yte</var>.

   </p>
      </li><li>
       <p>Set <var>i</var> to <var>i</var> + 1.
  </p>
     </li></ol>
   </li></ol>
   <p>"<var>input</var> <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-for=3D"byte seq=
uence" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" data-lt=3D"starts with|start =
with" id=3D"byte-sequence-starts-with">starts with</dfn>
<var>potentialPrefix</var>" can be used as a synonym for "<var>potentialPre=
fix</var> is a
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte-seque=
nce-prefix" id=3D"ref-for-byte-sequence-prefix">prefix</a> of <var>input</v=
ar>".

</p>
   <p>A <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#by=
te-sequence" id=3D"ref-for-byte-sequence=E2=91=A0=E2=91=A1">byte sequence</=
a> <var>a</var> is <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-ex=
port=3D"" id=3D"byte-less-than">byte less than</dfn> a <a data-link-type=3D=
"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte-sequence" id=3D"ref-for-b=
yte-sequence=E2=91=A0=E2=91=A2">byte sequence</a>
<var>b</var> if the following steps return true:

</p>
   <ol>
    <li>
     <p>If <var>b</var> is a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infr=
a.spec.whatwg.org/#byte-sequence-prefix" id=3D"ref-for-byte-sequence-prefix=
=E2=91=A0">prefix</a> of <var>a</var>, then return false.

 </p>
    </li><li>
     <p>If <var>a</var> is a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infr=
a.spec.whatwg.org/#byte-sequence-prefix" id=3D"ref-for-byte-sequence-prefix=
=E2=91=A1">prefix</a> of <var>b</var>, then return true.

 </p>
    </li><li>
     <p>Let <var>n</var> be the smallest index such that the <var>n</var>th=
 <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte" id=
=3D"ref-for-byte=E2=91=A8">byte</a> of
 <var>a</var> is different from the <var>n</var>th byte of <var>b</var>. (T=
here has to be such an
 index, since neither byte sequence is a prefix of the other.)

 </p>
    </li><li>
     <p>If the <var>n</var>th byte of <var>a</var> is less than the <var>n<=
/var>th byte of
 <var>b</var>, then return true.

 </p>
    </li><li>
     <p>Return false.
</p>
   </li></ol>
   <hr>
   <div class=3D"algorithm" data-algorithm=3D"isomorphic decode">
    <p>To <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D""=
 id=3D"isomorphic-decode">isomorphic decode</dfn> a <a data-link-type=3D"df=
n" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte-sequence" id=3D"ref-for-byte=
-sequence=E2=91=A0=E2=91=A3">byte sequence</a> <var>input</var>, return a
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string" id=
=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A8">string</a> whose <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" hre=
f=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string-code-point-length" id=3D"ref-for=
-string-code-point-length">code point length</a> is equal to <var>input</va=
r>=E2=80=99s
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte-seque=
nce-length" id=3D"ref-for-byte-sequence-length=E2=91=A1">length</a> and who=
se <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-po=
int" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=A0">code points</a> have the same
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-point=
-value" id=3D"ref-for-code-point-value">values</a> as the <a data-link-type=
=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte-value" id=3D"ref-for-b=
yte-value=E2=91=A0">values</a> of <var>input</var>=E2=80=99s <a data-link-t=
ype=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte" id=3D"ref-for-byte=
=E2=91=A0=E2=93=AA">bytes</a>, in
the same order.
</p>
   </div>
   <h3 class=3D"heading settled" data-level=3D"4.6" id=3D"code-points"><spa=
n class=3D"secno">4.6. </span><span class=3D"content">Code points</span><a =
class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-points"></a=
></h3>
   <p>A <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" d=
ata-lt=3D"code point|character" id=3D"code-point">code point</dfn> is a Uni=
code code point and is
represented as "U+" followed by four-to-six <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=
=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-upper-hex-digit" id=3D"ref-for-asc=
ii-upper-hex-digit=E2=91=A0">ASCII upper hex digits</a>, in the range U+000=
0 to
U+10FFFF, inclusive. A <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec=
.whatwg.org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=A1">code point</a>=
=E2=80=99s <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-for=3D"code point" data-dfn-=
type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" id=3D"code-point-value">value</dfn> is its
underlying number.

</p>
   <p>A <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#co=
de-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=A2">code point</a> may be followed=
 by its name, by its rendered form between parentheses when it
is not U+0028 or U+0029, or by both. Documents using the Infra Standard are=
 encouraged to follow
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-point=
" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=A3">code points</a> by their name when the=
y cannot be rendered or are U+0028 or U+0029; otherwise,
follow them by their rendered form between parentheses, for legibility.

</p>
   <p>A <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#co=
de-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=A4">code point</a>=E2=80=99s name =
is defined in <cite>Unicode</cite> and represented in
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-uppe=
rcase" id=3D"ref-for-ascii-uppercase=E2=91=A0">ASCII uppercase</a>. <a data=
-link-type=3D"biblio" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#biblio-unicode=
" title=3D"The Unicode Standard">[UNICODE]</a>

</p>
   <div class=3D"example" id=3D"example-code-point-notation">
    <a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-c=
ode-point-notation"></a>
=20
    <p>The <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/=
#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=A5">code point</a> rendered as =
=F0=9F=A4=94 is represented as U+1F914.

 </p>
    <p>When referring to that <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://inf=
ra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=A6">code poi=
nt</a>, we might say "U+1F914 (=F0=9F=A4=94)", to provide extra context.
 Documents are allowed to use "U+1F914 THINKING FACE (=F0=9F=A4=94)" as wel=
l, though this is somewhat verbose.
</p>
   </div>
   <p class=3D"example" id=3D"example-code-point-notation-hard-to-render"><=
a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-code-p=
oint-notation-hard-to-render"></a><a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https:/=
/infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=A7">Code=
 points</a> that are difficult
to render unambigiously, such as U+000A, can be referred to as "U+000A LF".=
 U+0029 can be referred
to as "U+0029 RIGHT PARENTHESIS", because even though it renders, this avoi=
ds unmatched parentheses.

</p>
   <p><a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code=
-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=A8">Code points</a> are sometimes re=
ferred to as <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.or=
g/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=A0=E2=93=AA">characters</a> a=
nd in certain contexts are
prefixed with "0x" rather than "U+".

</p>
   <p>A <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" i=
d=3D"leading-surrogate">leading surrogate</dfn> is a <a data-link-type=3D"d=
fn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-p=
oint=E2=91=A0=E2=91=A0">code point</a> that is in the range U+D800 to
U+DBFF, inclusive.

</p>
   <p>A <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" i=
d=3D"trailing-surrogate">trailing surrogate</dfn> is a <a data-link-type=3D=
"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code=
-point=E2=91=A0=E2=91=A1">code point</a> that is in the range U+DC00 to
U+DFFF, inclusive.

</p>
   <p>A <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" i=
d=3D"surrogate">surrogate</dfn> is a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"http=
s://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#leading-surrogate" id=3D"ref-for-leading-surroga=
te">leading surrogate</a> or a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://in=
fra.spec.whatwg.org/#trailing-surrogate" id=3D"ref-for-trailing-surrogate">=
trailing surrogate</a>.

</p>
   <p>A <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" i=
d=3D"scalar-value">scalar value</dfn> is a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=
=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=
=91=A0=E2=91=A2">code point</a> that is not a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" hre=
f=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#surrogate" id=3D"ref-for-surrogate">sur=
rogate</a>.

</p>
   <p>A <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" i=
d=3D"noncharacter">noncharacter</dfn> is a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=
=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=
=91=A0=E2=91=A3">code point</a> that is in the range U+FDD0 to U+FDEF,
inclusive, or U+FFFE, U+FFFF, U+1FFFE, U+1FFFF, U+2FFFE, U+2FFFF, U+3FFFE, =
U+3FFFF, U+4FFFE,
U+4FFFF, U+5FFFE, U+5FFFF, U+6FFFE, U+6FFFF, U+7FFFE, U+7FFFF, U+8FFFE, U+8=
FFFF, U+9FFFE, U+9FFFF,
U+AFFFE, U+AFFFF, U+BFFFE, U+BFFFF, U+CFFFE, U+CFFFF, U+DFFFE, U+DFFFF, U+E=
FFFE, U+EFFFF, U+FFFFE,
U+FFFFF, U+10FFFE, or U+10FFFF.

</p>
   <p>An <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" =
id=3D"ascii-code-point">ASCII code point</dfn> is a <a data-link-type=3D"df=
n" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-po=
int=E2=91=A0=E2=91=A4">code point</a> in the range U+0000 NULL to
U+007F DELETE, inclusive.

</p>
   <p>An <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" =
data-lt=3D"ASCII tab or newline|ASCII tabs or newlines" id=3D"ascii-tab-or-=
newline">ASCII tab or newline</dfn> is
U+0009 TAB, U+000A LF, or U+000D CR.

</p>
   <p><dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" id=
=3D"ascii-whitespace">ASCII whitespace</dfn> is U+0009 TAB, U+000A LF, U+00=
0C FF, U+000D CR, or U+0020
SPACE.

</p>
   <p class=3D"note" role=3D"note">"Whitespace" is a mass noun.

</p>
   <div class=3D"note" role=3D"note">
    <p>The XML, JSON, and parts of the HTTP specifications exclude U+000C F=
F in their definition of
 whitespace:

 </p>
    <ul class=3D"brief">
     <li><a href=3D"https://www.w3.org/TR/xml/#NT-S">XML=E2=80=99s S produc=
tion</a>
 =20
     </li><li><a href=3D"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc8259/#section-2=
">JSON=E2=80=99s ws production</a>
 =20
     </li><li><a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://fetch.spec.whatwg.o=
rg/#http-whitespace" id=3D"ref-for-http-whitespace">HTTP whitespace</a>
=20
    </li></ul>
    <p>Prefer using Infra=E2=80=99s <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https=
://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-whitespace" id=3D"ref-for-ascii-whitespace"=
>ASCII whitespace</a> definition for new features, unless your specificatio=
n
 deals exclusively with XML/JSON/HTTP.
</p>
   </div>
   <p>A <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" i=
d=3D"c0-control">C0 control</dfn> is a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"ht=
tps://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=A0=
=E2=91=A5">code point</a> in the range U+0000 NULL to
U+001F INFORMATION SEPARATOR ONE, inclusive.

</p>
   <p>A <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" d=
ata-lt=3D"C0 control or space|C0 controls or spaces" id=3D"c0-control-or-sp=
ace">C0 control or space</dfn> is a
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#c0-control=
" id=3D"ref-for-c0-control">C0 control</a> or U+0020 SPACE.

</p>
   <p>A <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" i=
d=3D"control">control</dfn> is a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://=
infra.spec.whatwg.org/#c0-control" id=3D"ref-for-c0-control=E2=91=A0">C0 co=
ntrol</a> or a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.=
org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=A0=E2=91=A6">code point</a>=
 in the range
U+007F DELETE to U+009F APPLICATION PROGRAM COMMAND, inclusive.

</p>
   <p>An <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" =
id=3D"ascii-digit">ASCII digit</dfn> is a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D=
"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=
=A0=E2=91=A7">code point</a> in the range U+0030 (0) to U+0039 (9),
inclusive.

</p>
   <p>An <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" =
id=3D"ascii-upper-hex-digit">ASCII upper hex digit</dfn> is an <a data-link=
-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-digit" id=3D"ref=
-for-ascii-digit">ASCII digit</a> or a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"ht=
tps://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=A0=
=E2=91=A8">code point</a> in the
range U+0041 (A) to U+0046 (F), inclusive.

</p>
   <p>An <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" =
id=3D"ascii-lower-hex-digit">ASCII lower hex digit</dfn> is an <a data-link=
-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-digit" id=3D"ref=
-for-ascii-digit=E2=91=A0">ASCII digit</a> or a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" h=
ref=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=
=E2=91=A1=E2=93=AA">code point</a> in the
range U+0061 (a) to U+0066 (f), inclusive.

</p>
   <p>An <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" =
id=3D"ascii-hex-digit">ASCII hex digit</dfn> is an <a data-link-type=3D"dfn=
" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-upper-hex-digit" id=3D"ref-f=
or-ascii-upper-hex-digit=E2=91=A1">ASCII upper hex digit</a> or
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-lowe=
r-hex-digit" id=3D"ref-for-ascii-lower-hex-digit">ASCII lower hex digit</a>=
.

</p>
   <p>An <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" =
id=3D"ascii-upper-alpha">ASCII upper alpha</dfn> is a <a data-link-type=3D"=
dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-=
point=E2=91=A1=E2=91=A0">code point</a> in the range U+0041 (A) to
U+005A (Z), inclusive.

</p>
   <p>An <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" =
id=3D"ascii-lower-alpha">ASCII lower alpha</dfn> is a <a data-link-type=3D"=
dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-=
point=E2=91=A1=E2=91=A1">code point</a> in the range U+0061 (a) to
U+007A (z), inclusive.

</p>
   <p>An <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" =
id=3D"ascii-alpha">ASCII alpha</dfn> is an <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=
=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-upper-alpha" id=3D"ref-for-ascii-u=
pper-alpha">ASCII upper alpha</a> or <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"http=
s://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-lower-alpha" id=3D"ref-for-ascii-lower-alp=
ha">ASCII lower alpha</a>.

</p>
   <p>An <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" =
id=3D"ascii-alphanumeric">ASCII alphanumeric</dfn> is an <a data-link-type=
=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-digit" id=3D"ref-for-=
ascii-digit=E2=91=A1">ASCII digit</a> or <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"=
https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-alpha" id=3D"ref-for-ascii-alpha">ASCI=
I alpha</a>.


</p>
   <h3 class=3D"heading settled" data-level=3D"4.7" id=3D"strings"><span cl=
ass=3D"secno">4.7. </span><span class=3D"content">Strings</span><a class=3D=
"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#strings"></a></h3>
   <p>A <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" d=
ata-lt=3D"string|JavaScript string" id=3D"string">string</dfn> is a sequenc=
e of
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#16-bit-uns=
igned-integer" id=3D"ref-for-16-bit-unsigned-integer">16-bit unsigned integ=
ers</a>, also known as <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" dat=
a-export=3D"" data-lt=3D"code unit" id=3D"code-unit">code units</dfn>. A
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string" id=
=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A0=E2=93=AA">string</a> is also known as a <a data=
-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string" id=3D"jav=
ascript-string">JavaScript string</a>.
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string" id=
=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A0=E2=91=A0">Strings</a> are denoted by double quo=
tes and monospace font.

</p>
   <p class=3D"example" id=3D"example-string-notation"><a class=3D"self-lin=
k" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-string-notation"></a>"<co=
de>Hello, world!</code>" is a string.

</p>
   <p class=3D"note" role=3D"note">This is different from how <cite>Unicode=
</cite> defines "code unit". In particular it
refers exclusively to how <cite>Unicode</cite> defines it for Unicode 16-bi=
t strings. <a data-link-type=3D"biblio" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.o=
rg/#biblio-unicode" title=3D"The Unicode Standard">[UNICODE]</a>

</p>
   <p>A <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#st=
ring" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A0=E2=91=A1">string</a> can also be interp=
reted as containing <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.wh=
atwg.org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=A1=E2=91=A2">code poin=
ts</a>, per the conversion
defined in <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://tc39.github.io/ecma262=
/#sec-ecmascript-language-types-string-type" id=3D"ref-for-sec-ecmascript-l=
anguage-types-string-type">The String Type</a> section of the JavaScript sp=
ecification. <a data-link-type=3D"biblio" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg=
.org/#biblio-ecma-262" title=3D"ECMAScript Language Specification">[ECMA-26=
2]</a>

</p>
   <p class=3D"note" role=3D"note">This conversion process converts surroga=
te pairs into their corresponding
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#scalar-val=
ue" id=3D"ref-for-scalar-value">scalar value</a> and maps any remaining sur=
rogates to their corresponding <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://in=
fra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=A1=E2=91=A3=
">code point</a>,
leaving them effectively as-is.

</p>
   <p class=3D"example" id=3D"example-javascript-string-in-code-points"><a =
class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-javascri=
pt-string-in-code-points"></a>A <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://i=
nfra.spec.whatwg.org/#string" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A0=E2=91=A2">strin=
g</a> consisting of the
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-unit"=
 id=3D"ref-for-code-unit">code units</a> 0xD83D, 0xDCA9, and 0xD800, when i=
nterpreted as containing <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.sp=
ec.whatwg.org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=A1=E2=91=A4">code=
 points</a>,
would consist of the <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.w=
hatwg.org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=A1=E2=91=A5">code poi=
nts</a> U+1F4A9 and U+D800.

</p>
   <p>A <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#st=
ring" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A0=E2=91=A3">string</a>=E2=80=99s
<dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-for=3D"string,JavaScript string,ASCII s=
tring,isomorphic string,scalar value string" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-exp=
ort=3D"" id=3D"string-length"><span class=3D"bs-old-id" id=3D"javascript-st=
ring-length"></span>length</dfn>
is the number of <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatw=
g.org/#code-unit" id=3D"ref-for-code-unit=E2=91=A0">code units</a> it conta=
ins.

</p>
   <p>A <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#st=
ring" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A0=E2=91=A4">string</a>=E2=80=99s
<dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-for=3D"string,JavaScript string,ASCII s=
tring,isomorphic string,scalar value string" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-exp=
ort=3D"" id=3D"string-code-point-length">code point length</dfn> is the num=
ber
of <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-po=
int" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=A1=E2=91=A6">code points</a> it contain=
s.

</p>
   <hr>
   <p>To signify <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatw=
g.org/#string" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A0=E2=91=A5">strings</a> with add=
itional restrictions on the <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra=
.spec.whatwg.org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=A1=E2=91=A7">c=
ode points</a> they can contain
this specification defines <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.=
spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-string" id=3D"ref-for-ascii-string">ASCII strings</a=
>, <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#isomorp=
hic-string" id=3D"ref-for-isomorphic-string">isomorphic strings</a>, and
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#scalar-val=
ue-string" id=3D"ref-for-scalar-value-string">scalar value strings</a>. Usi=
ng these improves clarity in specifications.

</p>
   <p>An <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" =
id=3D"ascii-string">ASCII string</dfn> is a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=
=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A0=E2=
=91=A6">string</a> whose <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.sp=
ec.whatwg.org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=A1=E2=91=A8">code=
 points</a> are all
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-code=
-point" id=3D"ref-for-ascii-code-point">ASCII code points</a>.

</p>
   <p>An <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" =
id=3D"isomorphic-string">isomorphic string</dfn> is a <a data-link-type=3D"=
dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=
=91=A0=E2=91=A7">string</a> whose <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https:/=
/infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=A2=E2=93=
=AA">code points</a> are all in the
range U+0000 NULL to U+00FF (=C3=BF), inclusive.

</p>
   <p>A <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" i=
d=3D"scalar-value-string">scalar value string</dfn> is a <a data-link-type=
=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string" id=3D"ref-for-strin=
g=E2=91=A0=E2=91=A8">string</a> whose <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"htt=
ps://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=A2=
=E2=91=A0">code points</a> are all
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#scalar-val=
ue" id=3D"ref-for-scalar-value=E2=91=A0">scalar values</a>.

</p>
   <p class=3D"note" role=3D"note">A <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"http=
s://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#scalar-value-string" id=3D"ref-for-scalar-value-=
string=E2=91=A0">scalar value string</a> is useful for any kind of I/O or o=
ther kind of operation
where <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://encoding.spec.whatwg.org/#u=
tf-8-encode" id=3D"ref-for-utf-8-encode=E2=91=A0">UTF-8 encode</a> comes in=
to play.

</p>
   <hr>
   <p>To <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-for=3D"string,JavaScript strin=
g" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" id=3D"javascript-string-convert">=
convert</dfn> a
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string" id=
=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A1=E2=93=AA">string</a> into a <a data-link-type=
=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#scalar-value-string" id=3D"=
ref-for-scalar-value-string=E2=91=A1">scalar value string</a>, replace any =
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#surrogate"=
 id=3D"ref-for-surrogate=E2=91=A0">surrogates</a> with U+FFFD (=EF=BF=BD).

</p>
   <div class=3D"note" role=3D"note">
    <p>The replaced surrogates are never part of surrogate pairs, since the=
 process of interpreting the
 string as containing <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.=
whatwg.org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=A2=E2=91=A1">code po=
ints</a> will have converted surrogate pairs into
 <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#scalar-va=
lue" id=3D"ref-for-scalar-value=E2=91=A1">scalar values</a>.

 </p>
    <p>A <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#s=
calar-value-string" id=3D"ref-for-scalar-value-string=E2=91=A2">scalar valu=
e string</a> can always be used as a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"http=
s://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A1=E2=91=A0">=
string</a> implicitly since every
 <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#scalar-va=
lue-string" id=3D"ref-for-scalar-value-string=E2=91=A3">scalar value string=
</a> is a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#=
string" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A1=E2=91=A1">string</a>. On the other ha=
nd, a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#stri=
ng" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A1=E2=91=A2">string</a> can only be
 implicitly used as a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.=
whatwg.org/#scalar-value-string" id=3D"ref-for-scalar-value-string=E2=91=A4=
">scalar value string</a> if it is known to not contain <a data-link-type=
=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#surrogate" id=3D"ref-for-su=
rrogate=E2=91=A1">surrogates</a>;
 otherwise a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.or=
g/#javascript-string-convert" id=3D"ref-for-javascript-string-convert">conv=
ersion</a> is to be performed.

 </p>
    <p>An implementation likely has to perform explicit conversion, dependi=
ng on how it actually ends
 up representing <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatw=
g.org/#string" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A1=E2=91=A3">strings</a> and <a d=
ata-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#scalar-value-s=
tring" id=3D"ref-for-scalar-value-string=E2=91=A5">scalar value strings</a>=
. It is fairly typical for
 implementations to have multiple implementations of <a data-link-type=3D"d=
fn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=
=91=A1=E2=91=A4">strings</a> alone for performance and memory
 reasons.
</p>
   </div>
   <hr>
   <p>A <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#st=
ring" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A1=E2=91=A5">string</a> <var>a</var> <dfn =
class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-for=3D"string" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-ex=
port=3D"" data-lt=3D"is|identical to" id=3D"string-is">is</dfn> or is
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string-is"=
 id=3D"ref-for-string-is">identical to</a> a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=
=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A1=E2=
=91=A6">string</a> <var>b</var> if it consists of the same sequence of
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-unit"=
 id=3D"ref-for-code-unit=E2=91=A1">code units</a>.

</p>
   <p>Except where otherwise stated, all string comparisons use <a data-lin=
k-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string-is" id=3D"ref-=
for-string-is=E2=91=A0">is</a>.

</p>
   <p class=3D"note" role=3D"note">This type of <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" h=
ref=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A1=
=E2=91=A7">string</a> comparison was formerly known as a "case-sensitive"
comparison in <cite>HTML</cite>. Strings that compare as <a data-link-type=
=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string-is" id=3D"ref-for-st=
ring-is=E2=91=A1">identical to</a> one another
are not only sensitive to case variation (such as UPPER and lower case), bu=
t also to other code
point encoding choices, such as normalization form or the order of combinin=
g marks. Two strings that
are visually or even canonically equivalent according to <cite>Unicode</cit=
e> might still not be
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string-is"=
 id=3D"ref-for-string-is=E2=91=A2">identical to</a> each other. <a data-lin=
k-type=3D"biblio" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#biblio-html" title=
=3D"HTML Standard">[HTML]</a> <a data-link-type=3D"biblio" href=3D"https://=
infra.spec.whatwg.org/#biblio-unicode" title=3D"The Unicode Standard">[UNIC=
ODE]</a>

</p>
   <p>A <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#st=
ring" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A1=E2=91=A8">string</a> <var>potentialPref=
ix</var> is a <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=
=3D"" id=3D"code-unit-prefix">code unit prefix</dfn> of a
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string" id=
=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A2=E2=93=AA">string</a> <var>input</var> if the fo=
llowing steps return true:

</p>
   <ol>
    <li>
     <p>Let <var>i</var> be 0.

 </p>
    </li><li>
     <p><a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#it=
eration-while" id=3D"ref-for-iteration-while=E2=91=A0">While</a> true:

  </p>
     <ol>
      <li>
       <p>If <var>i</var> is greater than or equal to <var>potentialPrefix<=
/var>=E2=80=99s
   <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string-=
length" id=3D"ref-for-string-length">length</a>, then return true.

   </p>
      </li><li>
       <p>If <var>i</var> is greater than or equal to <var>input</var>=E2=
=80=99s <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#st=
ring-length" id=3D"ref-for-string-length=E2=91=A0">length</a>,
   then return false.

   </p>
      </li><li>
       <p>Let <var>potentialPrefixCodeUnit</var> be the <var>i</var>th <a d=
ata-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-unit" id=
=3D"ref-for-code-unit=E2=91=A2">code unit</a> of
   <var>potentialPrefix</var>.

   </p>
      </li><li>
       <p>Let <var>inputCodeUnit</var> be the <var>i</var>th <a data-link-t=
ype=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-unit" id=3D"ref-for=
-code-unit=E2=91=A3">code unit</a> of <var>input</var>.

   </p>
      </li><li>
       <p>Return false if <var>potentialPrefixCodeUnit</var> is not <var>in=
putCodeUnit</var>.

   </p>
      </li><li>
       <p>Set <var>i</var> to <var>i</var> + 1.
  </p>
     </li></ol>
   </li></ol>
   <p>When it is clear from context that <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"=
https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-unit" id=3D"ref-for-code-unit=E2=91=A4"=
>code units</a> are in play, e.g., because one of the
strings is a literal containing only characters that are in the range U+002=
0 SPACE to U+007E (~),
"<var>input</var> <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-for=3D"string" data-d=
fn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" id=3D"string-starts-with">starts with</dfn=
> <var>potentialPrefix</var>" can be used
as a synonym for "<var>potentialPrefix</var> is a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn"=
 href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-unit-prefix" id=3D"ref-for-cod=
e-unit-prefix">code unit prefix</a> of <var>input</var>".

</p>
   <p class=3D"example" id=3D"code-unit-prefix-example"><a class=3D"self-li=
nk" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-unit-prefix-example"></a>Wi=
th unknown values, it is good to be explicit:
<var>targetString</var> is a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infr=
a.spec.whatwg.org/#code-unit-prefix" id=3D"ref-for-code-unit-prefix=E2=91=
=A0">code unit prefix</a> of <var>userInput</var>. But with a
literal, we can use plainer language: <var>userInput</var> <a data-link-typ=
e=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string-starts-with" id=3D"=
ref-for-string-starts-with">starts with</a>
"<code>!</code>".

</p>
   <p>A <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#st=
ring" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A2=E2=91=A0">string</a> <var>potentialSuff=
ix</var> is a <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=
=3D"" id=3D"code-unit-suffix">code unit suffix</dfn> of a
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string" id=
=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A2=E2=91=A1">string</a> <var>input</var> if the fo=
llowing steps return true:

</p>
   <ol>
    <li>
     <p>Let <var>i</var> be 1.

 </p>
    </li><li>
     <p><a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#it=
eration-while" id=3D"ref-for-iteration-while=E2=91=A1">While</a> true:

  </p>
     <ol>
      <li>
       <p>Let <var>potentialSuffixIndex</var> be <var>potentialSuffix</var>=
=E2=80=99s
   <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string-=
length" id=3D"ref-for-string-length=E2=91=A1">length</a> =E2=88=92 <var>i</=
var>.

   </p>
      </li><li>
       <p>Let <var>inputIndex</var> be <var>input</var>=E2=80=99s <a data-l=
ink-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string-length" id=
=3D"ref-for-string-length=E2=91=A2">length</a> =E2=88=92
   <var>i</var>.

   </p>
      </li><li>
       <p>If <var>potentialSuffixIndex</var> is less than 0, then return tr=
ue.

   </p>
      </li><li>
       <p>If <var>inputIndex</var> is less than 0, then return false.

   </p>
      </li><li>
       <p>Let <var>potentialSuffixCodeUnit</var> be the <var>potentialSuffi=
xIndex</var>th
   <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-un=
it" id=3D"ref-for-code-unit=E2=91=A5">code unit</a> of <var>potentialSuffix=
</var>.

   </p>
      </li><li>
       <p>Let <var>inputCodeUnit</var> be the <var>inputIndex</var>th <a da=
ta-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-unit" id=
=3D"ref-for-code-unit=E2=91=A6">code unit</a> of
   <var>input</var>.

   </p>
      </li><li>
       <p>Return false if <var>potentialSuffixCodeUnit</var> is not <var>in=
putCodeUnit</var>.

   </p>
      </li><li>
       <p>Set <var>i</var> to <var>i</var> + 1.
  </p>
     </li></ol>
   </li></ol>
   <p>When it is clear from context that <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"=
https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-unit" id=3D"ref-for-code-unit=E2=91=A7"=
>code units</a> are in play, e.g., because one of the
strings is a literal containing only characters that are in the range U+002=
0 SPACE to U+007E (~),
"<var>input</var> <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-for=3D"string" data-d=
fn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" id=3D"string-ends-with">ends with</dfn> <v=
ar>potentialSuffix</var>" can be used as
a synonym for "<var>potentialSuffix</var> is a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" hr=
ef=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-unit-suffix" id=3D"ref-for-code-u=
nit-suffix">code unit suffix</a> of <var>input</var>".

</p>
   <p class=3D"example" id=3D"code-unit-suffix-example"><a class=3D"self-li=
nk" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-unit-suffix-example"></a>Wi=
th unknown values, it is good to be explicit:
<var>targetString</var> is a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infr=
a.spec.whatwg.org/#code-unit-suffix" id=3D"ref-for-code-unit-suffix=E2=91=
=A0">code unit suffix</a> of <var>domain</var>. But with a
literal, we can use plainer language: <var>domain</var> <a data-link-type=
=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string-ends-with" id=3D"ref=
-for-string-ends-with">ends with</a>
"<code>.</code>".

</p>
   <hr>
   <p>A <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#st=
ring" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A2=E2=91=A2">string</a> <var>a</var> is <d=
fn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" id=3D"code-=
unit-less-than">code unit less than</dfn> a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=
=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A2=E2=
=91=A3">string</a>
<var>b</var> if the following steps return true:

</p>
   <ol>
    <li>
     <p>If <var>b</var> is a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infr=
a.spec.whatwg.org/#code-unit-prefix" id=3D"ref-for-code-unit-prefix=E2=91=
=A1">code unit prefix</a> of <var>a</var>, then return false.

 </p>
    </li><li>
     <p>If <var>a</var> is a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infr=
a.spec.whatwg.org/#code-unit-prefix" id=3D"ref-for-code-unit-prefix=E2=91=
=A2">code unit prefix</a> of <var>b</var>, then return true.

 </p>
    </li><li>
     <p>Let <var>n</var> be the smallest index such that the <var>n</var>th=
 <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-unit=
" id=3D"ref-for-code-unit=E2=91=A8">code unit</a> of
 <var>a</var> is different from the <var>n</var>th code unit of <var>b</var=
>. (There has to be such
 an index, since neither string is a prefix of the other.)

 </p>
    </li><li>
     <p>If the <var>n</var>th code unit of <var>a</var> is less than the <v=
ar>n</var>th code unit of
 <var>b</var>, then return true.

 </p>
    </li><li>
     <p>Return false.
</p>
   </li></ol>
   <p class=3D"note" role=3D"note">This matches the ordering used by JavaSc=
ript=E2=80=99s <code>&lt;</code> operator, and its
<code class=3D"idl"><a data-link-type=3D"idl" href=3D"https://tc39.github.i=
o/ecma262/#sec-array.prototype.sort" id=3D"ref-for-sec-array.prototype.sort=
">sort()</a></code> method on an array of strings. This ordering compares t=
he 16-bit code units in each
string, producing a highly efficient, consistent, and deterministic sort or=
der. The resulting
ordering will not match any particular alphabet or lexicographic order, par=
ticularly for
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-point=
" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=A2=E2=91=A2">code points</a> represented b=
y a surrogate pair. <a data-link-type=3D"biblio" href=3D"https://infra.spec=
.whatwg.org/#biblio-ecma-262" title=3D"ECMAScript Language Specification">[=
ECMA-262]</a>

</p>
   <p class=3D"example" id=3D"example-code-unit-less-than"><a class=3D"self=
-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#example-code-unit-less-than">=
</a>For example, the code point U+FF5E FULLWIDTH
TILDE (=EF=BD=9E) is obviously less than the code point U+1F600 (=F0=9F=98=
=80), but the tilde is composed of a single
code unit 0xFF5E, while the smiley is composed of two code units 0xD83D and=
 0XDE00, so the smiley is
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-unit-=
less-than" id=3D"ref-for-code-unit-less-than">code unit less than</a> the t=
ilde.

</p>
   <hr>
   <p>The <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D""=
 id=3D"code-unit-substring">code unit substring</dfn> from <var>start</var>=
 with length <var>length</var>
within a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#s=
tring" id=3D"ref-for-string=E2=91=A2=E2=91=A4">string</a> <var>string</var>=
 is determined as follows:

</p>
   <ol>
    <li>
     <p><a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#as=
sert" id=3D"ref-for-assert=E2=91=A1">Assert</a>: <var>start</var> and <var>=
length</var> are nonnegative.</p>
    </li><li>
     <p><a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#as=
sert" id=3D"ref-for-assert=E2=91=A2">Assert</a>: <var>start</var> + <var>le=
ngth</var> is less than or equal to
 <var>string</var>=E2=80=99s <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infr=
a.spec.whatwg.org/#string-length" id=3D"ref-for-string-length=E2=91=A3">len=
gth</a>.</p>
    </li><li>
     <p>Let <var>result</var> be the empty string.</p>
    </li><li>
     <p><a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#li=
st-iterate" id=3D"ref-for-list-iterate=E2=91=A3">For each</a> <var>i</var> =
of <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#the-exc=
lusive-range" id=3D"ref-for-the-exclusive-range">the range</a> from
 <var>start</var> to <var>start</var> + <var>length</var>, exclusive: appen=
d the <var>i</var>th
 <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-unit=
" id=3D"ref-for-code-unit=E2=91=A0=E2=93=AA">code unit</a> of <var>string</=
var> to <var>result</var>.</p>
    </li><li>
     <p>Return <var>result</var>.</p>
   </li></ol>
   <p>The <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D""=
