=91=A0">code unit substring</a> from 1 to 4.

</p>
   <p class=3D"note" role=3D"note">The numbers given to these algorithms ar=
e best thought of as positions
<em>between</em> <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatw=
g.org/#code-unit" id=3D"ref-for-code-unit=E2=91=A0=E2=91=A0">code units</a>=
, not indices of the code units themselves. The substring returned
is then formed by the code units between these positions. That explains why=
, for example, the
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-unit-=
substring-by-positions" id=3D"ref-for-code-unit-substring-by-positions=E2=
=91=A1">code unit substring</a> from 0 to 0 within the empty string
is the empty string, even though there is no code unit at index 0 within th=
e empty string.

</p>
   <p>The <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D""=
