   <h2 class=3D"heading settled" data-level=3D"7" id=3D"forgiving-base64"><=
span class=3D"secno">7. </span><span class=3D"content">Forgiving base64</sp=
an><a class=3D"self-link" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#forgiving-=
base64"></a></h2>
   <p>To <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" =
id=3D"forgiving-base64-encode">forgiving-base64 encode</dfn> given a <a dat=
a-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte-sequence" i=
d=3D"ref-for-byte-sequence=E2=91=A1=E2=91=A0">byte sequence</a> <var>data</=
var>, apply
the base64 algorithm defined in section 4 of RFC 4648 to <var>data</var> an=
d return the result.
<a data-link-type=3D"biblio" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#biblio-=
rfc4648" title=3D"The Base16, Base32, and Base64 Data Encodings">[RFC4648]<=
/a>

</p>
   <p class=3D"no-backref note" role=3D"note">This is named <a data-link-ty=
pe=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#forgiving-base64-encode" =
id=3D"ref-for-forgiving-base64-encode">forgiving-base64 encode</a> for symm=
etry with
<a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#forgiving-=
base64-decode" id=3D"ref-for-forgiving-base64-decode">forgiving-base64 deco=
de</a>, which is different from the RFC as it defines error handling for
certain inputs.

</p>
   <p>To <dfn class=3D"dfn-paneled" data-dfn-type=3D"dfn" data-export=3D"" =
id=3D"forgiving-base64-decode">forgiving-base64 decode</dfn> given a string=
 <var>data</var>, run these steps:</p>
   <ol>
    <li>
     <p>Remove all <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.wha=
twg.org/#ascii-whitespace" id=3D"ref-for-ascii-whitespace=E2=91=A6">ASCII w=
hitespace</a> from <var>data</var>.

 </p>
    </li><li>
     <p>If <var>data</var>=E2=80=99s <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"http=
s://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string-code-point-length" id=3D"ref-for-string-c=
ode-point-length=E2=91=A3">code point length</a> divides by 4 leaving no re=
mainder:

  </p>
     <ol>
      <li>
       <p>If <var>data</var> ends with one or two U+003D (=3D) <a data-link=
-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-=
for-code-point=E2=91=A4=E2=91=A2">code points</a>, then remove them
   from <var>data</var>.
  </p>
     </li></ol>
    </li><li>
     <p>If <var>data</var>=E2=80=99s <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"http=
s://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string-code-point-length" id=3D"ref-for-string-c=
ode-point-length=E2=91=A4">code point length</a> divides by 4 leaving a rem=
ainder of
 1, then return failure.

 </p>
    </li><li>
     <p>If <var>data</var> contains a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"htt=
ps://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=A4=
=E2=91=A3">code point</a> that is not one of

  </p>
     <ul class=3D"brief">
      <li>U+002B (+)
  =20
      </li><li>U+002F (/)
  =20
      </li><li><a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.whatwg.=
org/#ascii-alphanumeric" id=3D"ref-for-ascii-alphanumeric">ASCII alphanumer=
ic</a>
 =20
     </li></ul>
     <p>then return failure.

 </p>
    </li><li>
     <p>Let <var>output</var> be an empty <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D=
"https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#byte-sequence" id=3D"ref-for-byte-sequence=
=E2=91=A1=E2=91=A1">byte sequence</a>.

 </p>
    </li><li>
     <p>Let <var>buffer</var> be an empty buffer that can have bits appende=
d to it.

 </p>
    </li><li>
     <p>Let <var>position</var> be a <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"http=
s://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string-position-variable" id=3D"ref-for-string-p=
osition-variable=E2=91=A4">position variable</a> for <var>data</var>, initi=
ally
 pointing at the start of <var>data</var>.

 </p>
    </li><li>
     <p>While <var>position</var> does not point past the end of <var>data<=
/var>:

  </p>
     <ol>
      <li>
       <p>Find the <a data-link-type=3D"dfn" href=3D"https://infra.spec.wha=
twg.org/#code-point" id=3D"ref-for-code-point=E2=91=A4=E2=91=A4">code point=
</a> pointed to by <var>position</var> in the second column of
   Table 1: The Base 64 Alphabet of RFC 4648. Let <var>n</var> be the numbe=
r given in the first cell
   of the same row. <a data-link-type=3D"biblio" href=3D"https://infra.spec=
.whatwg.org/#biblio-rfc4648" title=3D"The Base16, Base32, and Base64 Data E=
ncodings">[RFC4648]</a>

   </p>
      </li><li>
       <p>Append the six bits corresponding to <var>n</var>, most significa=
nt bit first, to
   <var>buffer</var>.

   </p>
      </li><li>
       <p>If <var>buffer</var> has accumulated 24 bits, interpret them as t=
hree 8-bit big-endian
   numbers. Append three bytes with values equal to those numbers to <var>o=
utput</var>, in the same
   order, and then empty <var>buffer</var>.

   </p>
      </li><li>
       <p>Advance <var>position</var> by 1.
  </p>
     </li></ol>
    </li><li>
     <p>If <var>buffer</var> is not empty, it contains either 12 or 18 bits=
. If it contains 12 bits,
  then discard the last four and interpret the remaining eight as an 8-bit =
big-endian number. If it
  contains 18 bits, then discard the last two and interpret the remaining 1=
6 as two 8-bit big-endian
  numbers. Append the one or two bytes with values equal to those one or tw=
o numbers to
  <var>output</var>, in the same order.</p>
     <p class=3D"note" role=3D"note">The discarded bits mean that, for inst=
ance, "<code>YQ</code>" and
  "<code>YR</code>" both return `<code>a</code>`.

 </p>
    </li><li>
     <p>Return <var>output</var>.
</p>
   </li></ol>
